{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-66532",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{100\\,000}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{100\\,000}=\\frac{1}{{10}^{5}}={10}^{-5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{100\\,000})=\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{-5})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-5.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{-5})=-5\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
